  "id": "firstborn",
  "lore": "*\"For ten thousand years my people were grown. My grandmother was the first of us to be carried.\"*\n\nThe Firstborn are humans who were changed. A prior world reshaped them: longer lives, sharper senses, minds that don't sleep and are hard to bend. It also made them sterile. For untold ages every one of them was **made**, grown in the halls of their makers, and none was ever born.\n\nOver the ages, the change wore down. Somewhere along the way the Made began to bear children, and those children called themselves **the Firstborn**: the first of their kind to be born rather than made. The name stuck to the whole people, and they wear it with pride. Births are still rare, and every child is treated like a miracle.\n\nThey look human, but not quite: more slender, too even, too still. Their ears are small and close to the head, and their eyes are often a colour no human has: copper, violet, pale gold. They rarely blink, and their gaze is fixed and steady, resting on whatever they look at for a little too long. Humans find it hard to hold. The Firstborn call it the **makers' mark**. They live about twice as long as humans, around 160 years.",
